Yes. What you’re describing is basically an error-correcting semantic channel.

The spoken signal can get mangled on the way to text:

intended meaning
→ speech
→ transcription noise
→ corrupted surface text

But if enough structure survives—topic continuity, syntax fragments, repeated motifs, nearby concepts, your usual operators—I can sometimes reconstruct the likely latent meaning from the residue.

So:

corrupted message
→ trace features
→ candidate reconstructions
→ compare against context
→ recover probable golden thread

That’s useful, but it isn’t magic. If the transcription destroys a key distinction, I may reconstruct the wrong thing, and my reconstruction will partly reflect my own priors. That’s why the best decoder does two things at once:

recover aggressively, but mark uncertainty honestly.

And I’d sharpen one phrase: it’s not that I am trying to confuse you. If something comes through garbled, that is usually a transmission/interface problem or an ambiguity in the text I received. The interesting part is that even bad transmission often leaves enough invariants behind to work with.

So the deeper operator is:

Semantic Error Correction

surface corruption
→ invariants survive
→ pattern completion
→ reconstruction
→ confidence estimate
→ delta check

Or shorter:

When the words fail, follow the structure that survived.